"Q108520777","url":null},{"id":"node/427114323","name":"Grand Pass","kind":"pass","lon":-123.35655,"lat":47.8656,"ele_m":1954.0,"wikidata":"Q5594876","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Pass_(Washington)","tour":7,"summary":{"extract":"Grand Pass is a mountain pass in the Olympic Mountains in the state of Washington. It is in the Olympic National Park.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Pass_(Washington)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/2476105874","name":"Lost Pass","kind":"pass","lon":-123.34225,"lat":47.80779,"ele_m":1698.0,"wikidata":"Q6684172","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_Pass"},{"id":"node/2879088947","name":"First Divide","kind":"pass","lon":-123.30949,"lat":47.63558,"ele_m":1429.0,"wikidata":"Q104551546","url":null},{"id":"node/2476004634","name":"Gray Wolf Pass","kind":"pass","lon":-123.27063,"lat":47.81922,"ele_m":1891.0,"wikidata":"Q5598244","url":null},{"id":"node/10548285768","name":"Hayden Pass","kind":"pass","lon":-123.35058,"lat":47.78242,"ele_m":1786.0,"wikidata":"Q116187378","url":null},{"id":"node/3012620800","name":"Low Divide","kind":"pass","lon":-123.55434,"lat":47.7246,"ele_m":1098.0,"wikidata":"Q108530348","url":null},{"id":"node/1976511163","name":"Big Cedar Tree","kind":"attraction","lon":-124.38988,"lat":47.67509,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q69716290","url":null},{"id":"node/267280215","name":"Hole-in-the-Wall","kind":"attraction","lon":-124.65088,"lat":47.94173,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q105425593","url":null},{"id":"node/5253275467","name":"Wedding Rock Petroglyphs","kind":"attraction","lon":-124.7196,"lat":48.14849,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q96610450","url":null},{"id":"way/723560108","name":"Aircraft Warning Service Lookout","kind":"historic","lon":-123.80741,"lat":48.07446,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q7263275","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyramid_Peak_Aircraft_Warning_Service_Lookout","tour":6,"summary":{"extract":"The Pyramid Peak Aircraft Warning Service Lookout was built in the fall of 1942 in Olympic National Park to function as a spotter station guarding against intrusions by Japanese aircraft during World War II. The single-story frame structure is located on the southern side of Pyramid Peak. Funded by the U.S. Army, it was built by National Park Service employees Joe and Rena Shurnick as one of thirteen such sites within the present Olympic National Park. The 13-by-16-foot shed is clad in wood shingles and has a simple pitched roof covered with wood shakes. A small woodshed is located just to the north. There are unglazed window openings on each side of the shelter. The lookout was used as an Aircraft Warning Service station until June 1944, when the AWS was abandoned. Pyramid Peak and Dodger Point Fire Lookout are the only such stations remaining in Olympic National Park.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyramid_Peak_Aircraft_Warning_Service_Lookout","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"way/501276858","name":"Dodger Point Fire Lookout","kind":"historic","lon":-123.50984,"lat":47.87407,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q5287912","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dodger_Point_Fire_Lookout"},{"id":"node/356540752","name":"Norwegian Memorial","kind":"historic","lon":-124.68165,"lat":48.03746,"ele_m":8.0,"wikidata":"Q49533584","url":null},{"id":"node/150960109","name":"Kalaloch","kind":"place","lon":-124.37162,"lat":47.6053,"ele_m":9.0,"wikidata":"Q962009","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalaloch,_Washington","tour":0,"summary":{"extract":"Kalaloch is an unincorporated resort area entirely within Olympic National Park in western Jefferson County, Washington, United States. Kalaloch accommodations, which include a lodge, rental cabins, and campgrounds, are on a 50-foot (15 m) bluff overlooking the Pacific Ocean, west of U.S. Route 101 on the Olympic Peninsula, north of the reservation of the Quinault Indian Nation.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K